 #uuid "cb00b040-d99b-548d-bd9e-68e6d35aa259" :question/math-academy-id "q-31762" :question/problem "![](images/a2/a2e3d6a49f516b3037e0be9cb74312a59d2a924a6ec768a72660485951bb0302.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?"}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating Between Half-Open Intervals and Number Lines"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059829 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059388 :question/id #uuid "6d4bd0a2-0961-54c1-b781-b470d420d848" :question/math-academy-id "e-10816" :question/problem "Express the inequality $1 \\le x \\le4$ in interval notation." :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $1$ and $4$ *are* included.\n\n$$\n1\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{[}}1,4{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [1,4]\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f519fa9-de89-5b00-aa02-312cf8e32cba" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bde48c0-e5a3-5ec8-ae61-b4b406b5d038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[- 10, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056108} {:answer/id #uuid "a324f11c-84eb-5e97-81a6-e51cdd32f99d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-10, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056109} {:answer/id #uuid "c980cfab-047c-538b-8c86-405d0d88529d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- ∞, 3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056110} {:answer/id #uui